readings.c1s2:
{{smaller block|Green at the gate, which ''thrilled'' as we came out.
{{right|''Mrs. Browning.''}}}}

{{smaller block|All Nature with thy parting ''thrills,''
Like branches after birds new-flown.
{{right|''Lowell,'' To the Muse.}}}}

'''thrill'''<sup>1</sup> (thril), ''n.'' [In def. 1, < ME. ''thril,'' a transposed form of ''thirl''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.'' Cf. ''thrill''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.,'' for ''thirl''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.'' In the later senses, directly < ''thrill''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] '''1'''†. A hole; specifically, a breathing-hole; a nostril. Compare ''nostril'' (''nose-thrill'').

{{smaller block|With ''thrilles'' noght thrat but thriftily made,
Nawther to wyde ne to wan, but as hom well semyt.
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 3045.}}}}

{{smaller block|The bill of the dodo hooks and bends downwards; the ''thrill'' or breathing-place is in the midst.
{{right|''Sir T. Herbert,'' Travels, p. 383. (''Latham.'')}}}}

'''2.''' A subtle permeating influx of emotion or sensation; a feeling that permeates the whole system with subtle, irresistible force: as, a ''thrill'' of horror.

{{smaller block|A ''thrill'' of pity for the patient, and of gratitude for his services, which exaggerated, in her eyes, his good mien and handsome features. ''Scott,'' Quentin Durward, xv.}}

{{smaller block|The least motion which they made,
It seemed a ''thrill'' of pleasure. ''Wordsworth.''}}

{{smaller block|And I wait, with a ''thrill'' in every vein,
For the coming of the hurricane!
{{right|''Bryant,'' The Hurricane.}}}}

'''3.''' In ''med.,'' a peculiar tremor felt, in certain conditions of the respiratory or circulatory organs, upon applying the hand to the body; fremitus.—'''4.''' A throb; a beat or pulsation.

{{smaller block|Is it enough? or must I, while a ''thrill''
Lives in your sapient bosoms, cheat you still?
{{right|''Moore,'' Lalla Rookh, Veiled Prophet.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
¶
{{smaller block|The electric nerve, whose instantaneous ''thrill''
Makes next-door gossips of the antipodes.
{{right|''Lowell'', Agassiz, i. 1.}}}}

5. A tale or book the hearing or perusal of which sends a thrill or sensation of pleasure, pity, or excitement through one; a sensational story. [Slang, Eng.]

{{smaller block|That it should have been called by a name which rather reminds one of the sensational title of a shilling ''thrill'' seems to us a matter to be regretted.
{{right|''Westminster Rev.'', CXXVI. 382.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Hydatid thrill''', a vibration felt upon percussion of a hydatid tumor.—'''Purring thrill.''' See ''purr''<sup>1</sup>.}}

'''thrill'''<sup>2</sup> (thril), ''v. i.'' [A var. of ''trill''<sup>3</sup>, simulating ''thrill''<sup>1</sup>.] To warble; trill. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|The solemn harp's melodious warblings ''thrill.''
{{right|''Mickle'', tr. of Camoëns's Lusiad, ix. 783.}}}}

'''thrill'''<sup>2</sup> (thril), ''n.'' [See ''thrill''<sup>2</sup>, ''v.''] A warbling; a trill.

{{smaller block|Deafening the swallow's twitter, came a ''thrill''
Of trumpets. {{right|''Keats'', Lamia, ii.}}}}

{{smaller block|Carolling to her spinet with its thin metallic ''thrills.''
{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', Opening of the Piano.}}}}

{{smaller block|The starts and ''thrills''
Of birds that sang and rustled in the trees.
{{right|''R. W. Gilder'', The Poet's Fame.}}}}

'''thrillant†''' (thril′ant), ''a.'' [Irreg. < ''thrill''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ant.''] Piercing; thrilling.

{{smaller block|The knight his ''thrillant'' speare againe assayd.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., I. xi. 20.}}}}

'''thrilling'''<sup>1</sup> (thril′ing), ''p. a.'' 1†. Piercing; penetrating.

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|The species are known in general as ''thatch-palms'' in Jamaica. Two species occur in Florida: ''T. parviflora'', the taller, usually a small and very slender tree, becomes stemless in the pine-barrens in the variety ''Garberi''; the other, ''T. argentea'', the broom-palm of the Isthmus of Panama, is sometimes known in conservatories as ''chip-hat palm'', owing both names to the uses of its leaves. See also ''silk-top'' and ''silver-top palmetto'', under ''palmetto''.}}

'''thring†''' (thring), ''v.'' [< ME. ''thringen, thryngen'' (pret. ''thrang, throng'', pp. ''thrungen, throngen''), < AS. ''thringan'' (pret. ''thrang'', pp. ''thrungen''), thrust, press, = OS. ''thringan'' = D. ''dringen'' = MLG. ''dringen'', press, = OHG. ''dringan'', MHG. ''dringen'', press together, plait, weave, G. ''dringen, drängen'', press, etc., = Icel. ''thröngva, thryngva, threyngva'' = Sw. ''tränga'' = Dan. ''trænge'' = Goth. ''threihan'' (for *''thrinhen''), press, urge, trouble. Hence ult. ''throng''<sup>1</sup>. From the same ult. verb are also MHG. ''drihe'', an embroidering-needle, > ''drihen'', embroider; and perhaps E. ''thorough, through''<sup>1</sup>, and hence ''thirl''<sup>1</sup>, ''thrill''<sup>1</sup>.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To thrust; push; press.

{{smaller block|Whanne thou were in thraldom ''throng'',<br>And turmentid with many a iewe.
{{right|''Hymns to Virgin'', etc. (E. E. T. S.), p. 13.}}}}

{{smaller block|Who strengths the poor, and pridful men down ''thrings'',<br>And wracks at once the pow'rs of puissant kings.
{{right|''T. Hudson'', tr. of Du Bartas's Judith, iv.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To press; push; force one's way.

{{smaller block|Thruch the bodi ful neythe the hert<br>That gode swerd thruc him ''thrang''.
{{right|''Gy of Warwike'', p. 51. (''Halliwell.'')}}}}

{{smaller block|Mars . . . ne rested never stille,<br>But ''throng'' now her, now ther, among hem bothe.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Anelida and Arcite, l. 55.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
¶
'''thrip''' (thrip), ''n.'' [An abbr. of ''thrippence'', a pronunciation of ''three-pence''.] A threepenny piece. [Colloq.]

{{smaller block|He was not above any transaction, however small, that promised to bring him a dime where he had invested a ''thrip''.
{{right|''J. C. Harris'', Harper's Mag., LXXVI. 703.}}}}

'''Thripidæ''' (thrip′i-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Thrips'' + ''-idæ''.] The sole family of the order ''Thysanoptera'' (which see for characters). It was formerly considered as belonging to the ''Hemiptera''. Also called ''Thripsidæ''. See cut under ''Thrips''.

'''thripple†''', ''v. i.'' [Origin obscure.] To labor hard.

{{smaller block|Manie spend more at one of these wakesses than in all the whole yeer besides. This makes many a one to ''thripple'' & pinch, to runne into debte and daunger, and finallie brings many a one to vtter ruine and decay.
{{right|''Stubbes'', Anatomy of Abuses (ed. Furnivall), I. 153.}}}}

'''Thrips''' (thrips), ''n.'' [NL. (Linnæus, 1748), < L. ''thrips'', < Gr. θρίψ, a woodworm.] 1. The typical genus of the family ''Thripidæ'' or ''Thripsidæ''. {{smaller|The body is smooth and glabrous; the female has a four-valved decurved ovipositor. The species are numerous and wide-spread. ''T. striatus'' destroys onions in the United States.}}

2. [''l. c.''] (''a'') Any member of this genus or family, as ''Phlœothrips phylloxeræ'', which is said to feed on the leaf-gall form of the vine-pest. See cut in next column. (''b'') Among grape-growers, erroneously, any one of the leaf-hoppers of the

readings.c3s1:
homopterous family ''Jassidæ'', which feed on the grape. {{smaller|''Erythroneura vitis'' is the common grape-vine thrips, so-called, of the eastern United States. See cut under ''Erythroneura.''}}

'''Thripsidæ''' (thrip′si-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., irreg. < ''Thrips'' + ''-idæ.''] Same as ''Thripidæ.''

'''thrisle, thrissel''' (thris′l), ''n.'' Dialectal forms of ''thistle.''

'''thrist<sup>1</sup>†''', ''v.'' An obsolete form of ''thrust''<sup>1</sup>.

'''thrist<sup>2</sup>''' (thrist), ''n.'' and ''v.'' An obsolete or dialectal form of ''thirst.''

{{smaller block|Who shall him rew that swimming in the maine<br>Will die for ''thrist'', and water doth refuse?
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., II. vi. 17.}}}}

'''thristy''' (thris′ti), ''a.'' An obsolete or dialectal form of ''thirsty.'' ''Spenser'', F. Q., I. x. 38.

'''thritteen†''', ''a.'' and ''n.'' A Middle English form of ''thirteen.''

'''thrive''' (thrīv), ''v. i.''; pret. ''throve'' (sometimes ''thrived''), pp. ''thriven'' (sometimes ''thrived''), ppr. ''thriving.'' [< ME. ''thriven, thryven, thrifen'' (pret. ''throf, thraf'', pp. ''thriven''), < Icel. ''thrīfa'', clutch, grasp, grip, refl. ''thrīfask'', seize for oneself, thrive, = Norw. ''triva'', seize, refl. ''trivast'', thrive, = Sw. ''trifvas'' = Dan. ''trives'', refl., thrive.] 1. To prosper; flourish; be fortunate or successful.

{{smaller block|Thus he welke in the lande<br>With hys darte in his hande;<br>Under the wilde wodde wande<br>He wexe and wele ''thrafe.''
{{right|''Perceval'', l. 212. (''Halliwell.'')}}}}

{{smaller block|If I ''thrive'' well, I'll visit thee again.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of A., iv. 3. 170.}}}}

{{smaller block|For ought I see,}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|The lewdest persons ''thriue'' best, and are free
From punishment for sinne.
{{right|''Times' Whistle'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 5.}}}}

2. To increase in goods and estate; grow rich or richer; keep on increasing one's acquisitions.

{{smaller block|"Apparaile the propirli," quod Pride; . . .
"Late no poore neiȝbore ''thryue'' thee biside;
Alle other mennis councel loke thou dispise."
{{right|''Hymns to Virgin,'' etc. (E. E. T. S.), p. 62.}}}}

{{smaller block|Could fools to keep their own contrive,
On what, on whom, could gamesters ''thrive?''
{{right|''Gay,'' Pan and Fortune.}}}}

{{smaller block|And so she ''throve'' and prosper'd; so three years
She prosper'd. {{right|''Tennyson,'' Palace of Art.}}}}

3. To grow vigorously or luxuriantly; flourish.

{{smaller block|Let sette hem feete a sonder thries V,
Or twies X, as best is hem to ''thrive.''
{{right|''Palladius,'' Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 76.}}}}

{{smaller block|Love ''thrives'' not in the heart that shadows dreadeth.
{{right|''Shak.,'' Lucrece, l. 270.}}}}

{{smaller block|E'en the oak
''Thrives'' by the rude concussion of the storm.
{{right|''Cowper,'' Task, i. 378.}}}}

'''thriveless''' (thrīv′les), ''a.'' [< ''thrive'' + ''-less.''] Thriftless; unsuccessful; unprofitable. [Obsolete or archaic.]

{{smaller block|And thou, whose ''thriveless'' hands are ever straining
Earth's fluent breasts into an empty sieve.
{{right|''Quarles,'' Emblems, i. 12.}}}}

{{smaller block|The dull stagnation of a soul content,
Once foiled, to leave betimes a ''thriveless'' quest.
{{right|''Browning,'' Paracelsus.}}}}

'''thriven''' (thriv′n), ''p. a.'' 1. Past participle of ''thrive.''—2†. Grown.
